{
  "requirement": "CSS reports only `Comment` attachments. These include `/* … */` blocks and\n`//` line comments recognized by the bundled CSS grammar.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations. CSS must report only `Comment` attachments. Those must include `/* … */` blocks and `//` line comments that the bundled grammar recognizes.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax]` is marked `{test}` for `requirements.sdk.sources.css.attachments`. Its `test.css` case uses the source `/* block */\\na { color: red; }\\n// line\\n`. It collects every attachment from all outline items and sorts them by span start. It then asserts exact equality with `[(Comment, \"/* block */\"), (Comment, \"// line\")]`.\n\n- **Block and line comments:** both forms are exercised, and the assertion checks the kind and the exact text of each.\n- **Only `Comment`:** the assertion compares the full attachment list, with kinds. An `Annotation` or `Other` attachment in the CSS case would make it fail, and so would a missed comment.\n\nThe one thing I considered was an at-rule being misclassified as an `Annotation`. `Annotation` is only produced in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs#impl[Outline]#2/fn[from_legacy]`, from the legacy outline's annotations list. The same test's other cases cover the languages with real annotation syntax (Python decorators). The test does not assert that CSS with an at-rule yields no annotations, but the requirement does not call out at-rules. I do not count this as a gap.",
  "references": "7c29ed8ce72e8f0a30aa53f46b40dd74360105feb968625d1bede9d109faccd5",
